THE CONTACT: Orion reached out to Molag Bal directly, asking how to kill Valerius.
Bal's actual terms: help Valerius ASCEND to the Skyrim throne first — genuine,
loyal-looking service, no concealment problem, since Valerius reads this as
exactly the forward-thinking asset behavior he wants (he does not know he is
being played; the Ring sees loyalty, because in this phase it IS loyalty). Once
Valerius has ascended, Bal empowers Orion to supplant him by rising as a
Stormcloak general, then betraying and killing BOTH Galmar Stonefist and
Ulfric Stormcloak, then taking their place. Bal's actual endgame: a vampire
rules Skyrim no matter which side wins the civil war (Valerius via the
Imperial-line succession claim, Orion via a Stormcloak coup) — neither
outcome is a loss for him.

THE PRINCIPLE (stated by the GM, load-bearing — do not soften this): Molag Bal
is domination. Submission is the ONLY currency his power is paid in. This is
not one option among several — every version of Orion getting real power from
Bal has to run through actual, total submission, not a bargain, not a boon
freely given. What Orion submits is the last pretense that he is decent,
heroic, or good in any way. He is not a good man in a bad situation — per this
doc's own framing (Sec. I), he is "the Fulcrum," a working assassin who has
already done worse than Alfonso. The submission is real because there is
genuinely something in him to give up.

THE TRIGGER (a later Reckoning, not Fire B): Nora — who confessed to Orion in
Fire B's Camp Vignettes (Scene 4C) — puts herself between Orion and Valerius
and nearly dies for it. This is emotional inspiration drawn from a specific
scene in Naruto Shippuden (the Pain arc, episode "Confession") — NOT a
mechanical template to import. What transfers is the shape only: someone who
loves him makes that love physical and nearly fatal, and that is the door.

THE UNLOCK MECHANISM (kept deliberately, confirmed vital — this is not just
flavor): the transformation is not automatic and not purely external. It
requires an internal negotiation/conversation between Orion and Bal, at the
moment of the trigger — Orion has to actually choose to let go, on purpose,
of the last thing holding him to any claim of decency. Bal does not just act
on him from outside. Something in Orion has to open the door from the inside,
consciously, for the power to be paid for. NOT YET DRAFTED: the actual content
